  "assumptions": [
    "Illustration: a made-up two-setting loss bowl with simulated batch noise, not a real model trained on real data.",
    "Loss = (3x² + z²)/2, drawn at half height. It is steeper along x. Without noise, x swings and grows when the learning rate is above 2/3, so the top of the range (up to 1) diverges.",
    "Each step moves the ball by −learning rate × (true slope + noise). The noise spread is 2·√((64−b)/(63b)) for batch size b, so it is exactly zero at 64.",
    "Arrows are drawn at 3× the length of the step they show. Orange is the mini-batch step the ball takes. Grey is the step using the full-data slope. Both point downhill, opposite the slope.",
    "A run ends after 40 steps (finished). It ends early as diverged when the loss goes above 6 or the ball leaves the drawn bowl. The ball is then drawn at the bowl's edge, and the loss readout keeps the true value.",
    "The noise comes from a fixed seed. A new run continues the noise sequence, and Reset replays it from the start. While running, the ball takes 5 steps per second at 1× speed."
  ]
}